<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2013/05/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>einsum</id>
    <version>$version$</version>
    <authors>Sayan Samanta</authors>
    <license type="expression">BSL-1.0</license>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/reach2sayan/einsum</projectUrl>
    <repository type="git" url="https://github.com/reach2sayan/einsum.git" />
    <readme>README.md</readme>
    <description>Einstein summation for C++23: one immutable object, one call
operator, the result back by value in the operands' own type, every failure
through std::expected and nothing in any header that throws. The subscript can
reach a template parameter -- where a bad one is a static_assert carrying the
sentence rather than a template backtrace, and the contraction path is chosen
during constant evaluation -- or be parsed at run time. Native x64 package: the
public headers plus einsum_rt.dll and einsum_rt.lib in Release and Debug, with
the Boost 1.92, Eigen 3.4 and kokkos/mdspan header subsets the public interface
reaches bundled in, so a consumer needs no other package. Consumers compile with
/std:c++latest and need MSVC 19.38 (Visual Studio 17.8) or newer for
&lt;expected&gt;, std::views::enumerate and std::from_range. The binaries require
AVX2. Only the run-time subscript parser is compiled; the rest of the library,
the whole compile-time planner included, is headers.</description>
    <tags>native einsum tensor tensor-contraction linear-algebra eigen numpy compile-time constexpr cpp cpp23</tags>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="build\native\**" target="build\native" />
    <file src="README.md" target="" />
    <file src="LICENSE.txt" target="" />
  </files>
</package>
